    "evidence_text": "from Wikipedia articles, and Task 2 was speculation scope resolution from the BioScope Corpus.\n\nFor each task, the participants were provided the train and test set, which is henceforth referred to as Task 1B CoNLL and Task 2 CoNLL throughout this paper.\n\nFor our experimentation, we use the sub corpora of the BioScope Corpus (), namely the BioScope Abstracts sub corpora, which is referred to as BA, and the BioScope Full Papers sub corpora, which is referred to as BF.\n\nThis is because of the transfer learning capabilities of the models and the fact that BF is a smaller dataset than BA (BF: 2670 sentences, BA: 11871 sentences).\n\n4 datasets (BF, BA, SFU and Sherlock).\n\nWe work with the base variants of the models, while most results are reported with the large variants of the models.\n\nWhile training on multiple datasets, we observed a gain of 10.6 F1 points on BF and 1.94 F1 points on BA on the speculation detection task and 2.16 F1 points on BF and 0.25 F1 points on SFU on the scope resolution task over the single dataset training approach.",
